# Survey — a small game built on Core, written entirely in Compass.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE IS THE ENGINE'S DOOR, AND NOTHING ELSE. Every function the engine calls is here, and
# each one hands the work to the model that owns it. The engine only ever looks at `Rules`, so a game
# that put its whole self in this file would be a game in one file. The folders beside it are how
# this one is organized:
#
#   declare/    what the game IS, said once before the world exists
#   rules/      what happens to somebody, said every time it does
#   ranks.cm    a lookup this game owns and nothing else has an opinion about
#
# Every .cm under the scripts folder is one module, however deep it sits. Folders organize; they do
# not divide. A model in one file reaches a model in another by name.

shared model Rules

    # Declaring runs once, before the world exists. Everything it says is about what the game IS, never
    # about anybody in it — there is nobody in it yet.
    public function Configure(Builder game)
        Surveyor.Declare(game);
        Book.Declare(game);

        # Nothing in the world triggers resting; it happens as time passes. Once a second rather
        # than once a tick, because a point a second is the rate this game wants.
        game.TickEvery(20);
    end function

    public function OnPlayerJoined(Player who)
        Surveyor.Enroll(who);
    end function

    public function OnPlayerMoved(Player who, integer fromX, integer fromY)
        Walking.Stepped(who);
    end function

    # Once for each body in the world, every twentieth tick. A script has no other way to walk the
    # roster, so resting is written per-player rather than as a sweep.
    public function OnPlayerTick(Player who)
        Recovery.Rest(who);
    end function

    # `on` names whoever the verb was used on, and is blank for one offered on a square or on the HUD.
    # `picked` is the line of a panel's list that was selected, which this game never asks for.
    public function OnAction(Player who, string action, string on, integer map, integer x, integer y,
                             string picked)
        Notes.Did(who, action, on);
    end function

    # A form the surveyor filled in on the field book and sent. `values` carries the fields the
    # message's own model named, in the shapes it declared them.
    public function OnMessage(Player who, string message, Values values)
        Notes.Recorded(who, message, values);
    end function

    # Nothing dies on a botanical survey. Core's death path exists and works; this game says it
    # never runs.
    public string function OnMayDie(Player who, string cause)
        yield "Nobody dies out here.";
    end function

    # A dropped line is not a lost afternoon. Long enough that reconnecting puts a surveyor back
    # where they were, short enough that no one is left standing in a meadow overnight.
    public integer function OnLinger(Player who)
        yield 30;
    end function

end model
